  ⚠ THIS TEST NOW RETURNS ZERO - and that is the answer, not a failure.
  Under the OLD windows (survey year + the 5 before it) keeping precipitation
  truncated MORA's legacy temperature to ONE usable year, and dropping it
  recovered five - worth up to 1.28 degC on a single variable. The 2026-08-13
  windows (past = 1979-1989 at both sites) removed that cost entirely: every
  variable now has 10-11 complete legacy years whether precipitation is kept
  or not.

  CONSEQUENCE FOR STEP 3: the strongest argument for dropping precipitation -
  'it costs MORA its temperature record' - is SUPERSEDED. The remaining
  arguments stand on their own and must be judged without it:
    - TraCE21k resolves WP and MWT worst (lag-1 autocorrelation 0.15 / 0.04)
    - configured paleo constraints must remain within calibration ranges,
      but pond-to-survey geography still contributes to projected offsets
      (R/diagnostics/test_paleo_anthro_climate_match.R)
    - every window-selection anomaly that survives detrending is a
      precipitation one (R/diagnostics/test_survey_year_anomaly.R)
  Against them: the literature wants SNOWPACK, which needs MWT and WP.

